Transition syntax:
transition: property duration timing-function delay;
e.g. transition: background-color 0.3s ease-in-out;
or the shorthand transition: all 0.2s ease; to transition every animatable property that changes (use sparingly — 'all' can have performance costs if many properties change at once).

Animation syntax requires two parts:
1. A @keyframes block defining the stages:
@keyframes fadeIn {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to { opacity: 1; }
}
(or use percentage stops like 0%, 50%, 100% for more than two stages)

2. The animation property applying that keyframe block to an element:
animation: fadeIn 1s ease-in forwards;

Common timing functions for both: ease, ease-in, ease-out, ease-in-out, linear, or a custom cubic-bezier() curve. The animation-iteration-count property controls looping (e.g. infinite), and animation-fill-mode: forwards keeps the element at its final keyframe state after the animation completes, rather than snapping back to its original styles.
